<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8" ?>

<section xml:id="section-algebra">
<title>Algebra of Transformations</title>

<subsection>
	<title>Explore</title>

	<definition>
		<title>Translation</title>
		<statement><p>An transformation <m>T</m> is a translation if and only if there exists a non-zero constant vector <m>\vec{v}</m> such that <m>T(P)-P=\vec{v}</m> for all points <m>P.</m></p></statement>
	</definition>

	<definition>
		<title>Rotation</title>
		<statement><p>An transformation <m>T</m> is a rotation if and only if there exists a fixed point <m>C</m> and constant angle <m>\alpha</m> such that <m>m\angle PCT(P) = \alpha </m> and <m>\|\overline{CP}\| = \|\overline{CT(P)}\|</m> for all points <m>P</m></p></statement>
	</definition>

	<definition>
		<title>Reflection</title>
		<statement><p>An transformation <m>T</m> is a reflection if and only if there exists a fixed line <m>\ell</m> such that the line perpendicular to <m>\ell</m> through <m>P</m> contains <m>T(P)</m> and the distances from <m>P</m> and <m>T(P)</m> to the <m>\ell</m> are equal.</p></statement>
	</definition>

	<theorem>
		<statement>
			<p>Translations, rotations, and reflections are isometries.</p>
		</statement>
		  <proof>
			<p>
			<alert>Translation:</alert> Let <m>T</m> be a translation. By definition,
			there exists a vector <m>\mathbf{v}\neq\mathbf{0}</m> such that
			<md>
				T(P)-P=\mathbf{v}=T(Q)-Q.
			</md>
			This implies that there exists some vector <m>\mathbf{u}</m> so that
			<md>T(P)-T(Q) = P-Q=\mathbf{u}.</md>
			Taking the magnitude of <m>\mathbf{u}</m>, we get
			<md>||T(P)-T(Q)||=||P-Q||=||\mathbf{u}||.</md>
			By definition, <m>T</m> is an isometry.
			</p>
		</proof>
		<proof>
		<p>
			We have two points <m>P</m> and <m>Q</m> that is rotated around the point <m>C</m> making the new points <m>T(P)</m> and <m>T(Q)</m>. 
			<image source="images/geogebra-export-20"/>
			By the definition of rotation, <m>m\angle PCT(P) = m\angle QCT(Q)</m>. We can make triangles <m>\triangle T(Q)CT(P)</m> and <m>\triangle QCP</m>
			<image source="images/geogebra-export-21"/>
			With angle addition postulate, <m>m\angle T(Q)CT(P) = m\angle T(Q)CQ + m\angle QCT(P)</m> and <m>m\angle QCP = m\angle QCT(P) + m\angle T(P)CP</m>. Since <m>m\angle T(Q)CQ = m\angle T(P)CP</m>, we have that <m>\angle T(Q)CT(P) \cong \angle QCP</m> and by SAS, <m>\triangle T(Q)CT(P) \cong \triangle QCP</m>, making <m>\overline{T(Q)T(P)} \cong \overline{QP}</m>. Meaning their distance are equal making it an isometry.
		</p>
		</proof>
		<proof>
			<p>
			Take points <m>A,\ B</m> and a reflection <m>T</m> about the line
			<m>h</m>. We then have two cases:
			</p>

			<p>
			Case 1: <m>A</m> and <m>B</m> are colinear.
			</p>

			<p><image source="images/geometries-4-3-4-image-1"/></p>

			<p>
			Then, calling <m>h\cap\overleftrightarrow{AB}=C</m>, we have in the image above and from the definition of a reflection
			<m>\overline{AC}=\overline{A^{\prime}C}</m>,
			<m>\overline{BC}=\overline{B^{\prime}C}</m>, so:
			</p>

			<p>
			<md>
				<mrow>|\overline{AB}|=|\overline{AC}|-|\overline{BC}| = </mrow>
				<mrow>|\overline{A^{\prime}C}|-|\overline{B^{\prime}C}| =</mrow>
				<mrow>|\overline{A^{\prime}B^{\prime}}|</mrow>
			</md>
			</p>

			<p>
			Case 2: <m>A</m> and <m>B</m> are not colinear.
			</p>

			<p><image source="images/geometries-4-3-4-image-2"/></p>

			<p>
			By the definition of a reflection,
			<m>|\overline{AC}|=|\overline{A^{\prime}C}|</m> and
			<m>|\overline{BD}|=|\overline{B^{\prime}D}|</m>. We know from the
			definition of a reflection that
			<m>\overline{AA^{\prime}},\ \overline{BB^{\prime}}\perp\overline{CD}</m>.
			From these, we know that <m>\triangle{BCD}\cong\triangle{B^{\prime}CD}</m>
			by SAS. Hence <m>|\overline{BC}|=|\overline{B^{\prime}C}|</m>. We can also
			know that <m>\angle{ACB}\cong\angle{A^{\prime}CB^{\prime}}</m> from the
			following:
			</p>

			<p>
			<md>
				<mrow>90= \amp m(\angle{ACD})</mrow>
				<mrow>= \amp m(\angle{ACB})+m(\angle{BCD})</mrow>
				<intertext>and</intertext>
				<mrow>90= \amp m(\angle{A^{\prime}CD})</mrow>
				<mrow>= \amp m(\angle{A^{\prime}CB^{\prime}})+m(\angle{B^{\prime}CD}).</mrow>
			</md>
			</p>

			<p>
			These two give:
			<md>
				m(\angle{ACB})+m(\angle{BCD})=m(\angle{A^{\prime}CB^{\prime}})+m(\angle{B^{\prime}CD}).
			</md>
			Applying our triangle congruence shows:
			<md>
				m(\angle{ACB})=m(\angle{A^{\prime}CB^{\prime}}).
			</md>
			Therefore <m>\triangle{ACB}\cong\triangle{A^{\prime}CB^{\prime}}</m> by
			SAS.
			</p>

			<p>
			Hence <m>\overline{AB}\cong\overline{A^{\prime}B^{\prime}}</m> and we have
			proven that reflections are isometries.
			</p>
		</proof>
	</theorem>

	<exercise>
		<statement>
			<p>Draw an arbitrary triangle <m>ABC.</m> Draw the result <m>\triangle A'B'C'</m> of some translation. Draw the result <m>\triangle A''B''C''</m> of some translation applied to <m>\triangle A'B'C'.</m> Determine which type of isometry would transform <m>\triangle ABC </m> to <m>\triangle A''B''C''.</m></p>
		</statement>
			<solution>
				<p><image width="75%" source="images/triangle_transform_identify.png"/></p>
				<p>
				The transformation from <m>\triangle ABC</m> to <m>\triangle A''B''C''</m> would require a translation with the vector as pictured (vector addition). There is a special case when the translation vector for <m>\triangle ABC</m> to <m>\triangle A'B'C'</m> is <m>\vec{v}</m> and the translation vector for <m>\triangle A'B'C'</m> to <m>\triangle A''B''C''</m> is <m>-\vec{v}</m>. The vector for the transformation from <m>\triangle ABC</m> to <m>\triangle A''B''C''</m> is the zero vector and the transformation is the identity. 
				</p>
			</solution>
	</exercise>

	<exercise>
		<statement>
			<p>Complete the following table of composition of isometries.</p>
		</statement>
	</exercise>

	<exercise>
		<statement>
			<p>What is the composition of a reflection and a rotation?</p>
		</statement>
		<solution>
			<p><image source="images/ref-rot"/></p>

			<p>
			First, rotate <m>\triangle ABC</m> about point <m>X</m> with rotation
			angle <m>\alpha</m> counterclockwise to obtain the rotated triangle
			<m>\triangle A'B'C'</m>. Then, reflect <m>\triangle A'B'C'</m> across line
			<m>\overleftrightarrow{YZ}</m> to get triangle <m>\triangle A''B''C''</m>.
			To get from the original triangle <m>\triangle ABC</m> to the result under
			the two isometries <m>\triangle A''B''C''</m>, we must notice a few
			things.
			</p>

			<p>
			First, we cannot get from <m>\triangle ABC</m> to
			<m>\triangle A''B''C''</m> from a single translation since it is not
			always the case that <m>||\overline{AA''}||=||\overline{BB''}||</m>.
			Second, we cannot get from the original triangle to the resultant triangle
			with a single reflection since the perpendicular bisector of
			<m>\overline{AA''}</m> is not the perpendicular bisector of
			<m>\overline{BB''}</m>. Third, we cannot get from the first triangle to
			the final triangle with a single rotation since the chirality of each do
			not match. That is, the vertices of the first triangle are ABC clockwise
			but are <m>A''C''B''</m> on the final triangle.
			</p>

			<p>
			To get from the final triangle to the initial triangle, we first reflect
			<m>\triangle A''B''C''</m> across line <m>\overleftrightarrow{DZ'}</m>,
			where <m>m\angle ZDZ'=\alpha/2</m> clockwise. Then, we translate up the
			resultant triangle <m>\triangle A'''B'''C'''</m> to get back to the
			initial triangle <m>\triangle ABC</m>.
			</p>
		</solution>
	</exercise>

	<exercise>
		<statement>
			<p>What is the composition of a reflection and a translation?</p>
		</statement>
		<solution>
			<p>
				Doing the isometries Translation then Rotation is equivalent to doing a single rotation around a different point
			</p>
			<p>
			From observations in GeoGebra, we can easily show that unless the
			reflecting line is perpendicular to the translating vector, the
			translation and reflection do not commute.
			</p>
		</solution>
	</exercise>

	<table label="table-composition-isometries">
		<title>Composition of Isometries</title>
		<tabular>
			<row bottom="medium"><cell right="medium"></cell> <cell right="medium">Translate </cell> <cell right="medium"> Reflect </cell> <cell right="medium"> Rotate </cell> </row>
			<row bottom="medium"><cell right="medium">Translate </cell> <cell right="medium"> </cell> <cell right="medium"> </cell> <cell right="medium"> </cell> </row>
			<row bottom="medium"><cell right="medium">Reflect </cell> <cell right="medium"> </cell> <cell right="medium"> </cell> <cell right="medium"> </cell> </row>
			<row bottom="medium"><cell right="medium">Rotate </cell> <cell right="medium"> </cell> <cell right="medium"> </cell> <cell right="medium"> </cell> </row>
		</tabular>
	</table>
</subsection>

<subsection>
	<title>Answer and Explain</title>

	<exercise>
		<statement>
			<p>How many isometry types are there?</p>
		</statement>
	</exercise>

	<exercise>
		<statement>
			<p>How many isometry types are needed to generate all isometry types?</p>
		</statement>
	</exercise>

	<exercise>
		<statement>
			<p>How many isometries are needed to generate all isometries?</p>
		</statement>
	</exercise>

</subsection>

</section>
